Mixed licensing
================

This project mixes two licenses at source level.

LuOS code — MIT, the license above. Every file written for LuOS carries an
"SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT" header. This covers:

  src/                 LuOS kernel, components, drivers, filesystems, console and
                       games (src/lua/ is the third-party Lua interpreter — see below)
  include/ lib/        freestanding headers and the libc replacement
  linux/kpi/           the LinuxKPI runtime written for LuOS
                       (include/asm/agp.h is a verbatim Linux header and keeps GPL-2.0)
  linux/gen/           generated KPI glue for the GPU drivers
  linux/tools/         KPI build helpers (lkcc, lkadd, lkgen, kbuild-objs.mk)
  linux/linux.mk, linux/sync-upstream.py, linux/verify-upstream.sh
  userspace/           built-in filesystem builder (sysroot), the Mesa executor host
                       (mesa), the DRM/KMS test programs (drm) and the ring-3 test
                       programs (tests)
  scripts/             linker script and build helpers
  micropython/ports/luos/   the LuOS port of MicroPython
  Makefile, unpack.py

Linux code — GPL-2.0. linux/upstream/ is the Linux 6.18 kernel, taken verbatim;
every file keeps its original copyright headers and SPDX identifiers.

Third-party code — their own licenses, listed in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md:

  src/lua/             Lua (MIT)
  micropython/         MicroPython (MIT), except ports/luos/ which is LuOS code

Warning: the compiled kernel is GPL
===================================

The split above only exists at source level. LuOS compiles all of this code into a
single kernel binary, and that binary is a derivative work of the GPL-2.0 Linux code
in linux/upstream/. When you compile this project, the result as a whole becomes
GPL-2.0: the MIT parts remain MIT as source, but the compiled image may only be
redistributed under the terms of GPL-2.0. The mixing is possible because the MIT
license is GPL-compatible. Keep the split in mind when you reuse anything: LuOS MIT
files stay MIT wherever you copy them, while any binary that includes linux/upstream/
code is a GPL-2.0 work.
